this morning a u.s navy nuclear engineer  is accused of trying to sell  confidential information  to a foreign country authorities say the  suspect's contact person was not a  foreign agent however but an fbi agent  senior investigative correspondent  catherine herridge is at the pentagon  for us catherine good morning to you  every detail of this story is out of a  bad movie but sounds like a pretty good  piece of work from law enforcement good  morning  well tony this is an extraordinary  national security case that combines  allegations of spying nuclear submarine  secrets and a peanut butter sandwich  according to court documents in april  last year navy nuclear engineer jonathan  toby of annapolis maryland sent  documents to an unnamed foreign  government to sell sensitive information  according to the criminal complaint the  package was later obtained by the fbi  which began a months-long surveillance  operation in which an undercover agent  paid toby thousands in cryptocurrency in  exchange for information about the us's  virginia-class nuclear-powered attack  submarines toby was arrested saturday in  west virginia along with his school  teacher wife diana who prosecutors say  was acting as a lookout after he  allegedly went to a secret location  known in the spy world as a dead drop  and left a memory card with restricted  data wrapped in plastic and placed  between two slices of bread on half of a  peanut butter sandwich the half sandwich  was housed inside of a plastic bag so  far there's been no public comment from  the couple who are due in court tomorrow  gail  never thought that a peanut butter  sandwich would be considered ominous  i know that's a wild story thank you  catherine you're welcome we
 

